new file mode 100644
index 0000000..aa4b16a
--- /dev/null
+++ b/docs/privacy-audit.md
@@ -0,0 +1,34 @@
+# Privacy and integration review
+
+This is a technical inventory and release checklist, not an independent third-party certification. No vendor audit evidence was supplied with the project.
+
+| Integration | Previous exposure | Current decision |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| Cloud Functions analytics client | Every visitor's page and interaction data; script access to the DOM | Removed; no analytics collection |
+| Ad network queue / client | Page DOM and potential tracking | Disabled; no queue or remote script |
+| Google Fonts | Visitor IP, request metadata | Removed; local serif fallback |
+| Imgur article images | Visitor IP and potentially cookies | Replaced with existing local photographs; credentialed image loading removed |
+| Cinemai image host | Visitor request metadata | Replaced with an existing local photograph |
+| Cloud Functions account API | Username and password sent directly from browser | Disabled; forms collect no credentials |
+| Application hosting | IP/request metadata needed for delivery | Required; operator must document infrastructure logs, access, deletion and retention |
+| npm dependencies | Supply-chain/build risk | Lockfile pinned; automated production vulnerability audit required |
+
+## Before introducing or restoring a vendor
+
+The release owner must record a dated review identifying the provider, review owner, exact domains/endpoints, data fields, purpose, storage region, subprocessors, contractual protections, retention and automated deletion. Obtain available independent security assessment reports (for example a current SOC 2 report or penetration test), evaluate findings and remediation, and record evidence references rather than treating a certification as a guarantee. Reassess at least quarterly and whenever endpoints, vendor ownership, collection, or purpose change.
+
+For authentication, require a maintained backend, server-side validation and rate limits, password hashing, account export/deletion, short-lived server-side sessions and session rotation, secret management, generic error responses, and redacted logs. Do not restore the legacy credential demo or use browser storage for tokens. Optional analytics and embeds need prior, specific opt-in and withdrawal; Global Privacy Control should prevent optional tracking even after a stored opt-in. Review cookie partitioning if cross-site embeds are introduced.
+
+Run `pnpm build`, `pnpm test:privacy`, `pnpm audit --prod`, and `pnpm audit:privacy` for each release. The HTTP checks exercise request origin protection, bounded JSON payloads, guest behavior, data deletion, cookie flags, CSP hashes and same-origin resources without a browser. The source audit fails on remote resources or browser credential/storage patterns; investigate new failures rather than adding broad exemptions. Any approved vendor needs a new documented review and explicit configuration changes.
+
+## Deployment boundary
+
+Enforce HTTPS and rate limits at the ingress; return the same security headers for static assets and infrastructure error responses because Astro middleware handles application responses only. Restrict deployment logs to operational fields (method, route template, status, duration), redact query strings, cookies, tokens, IPs and request bodies, and set the shortest justified retention with access controls. Configure encryption and access controls for any future persistent store and its backups.
+
+Reading lists use bounded in-process memory (10,000 sessions, 100 article IDs per list) rather than disk. Expired entries are purged every minute, and reads also purge them. Restarts, capacity eviction and routing across replicas can clear a list early. If durable lists become necessary, use a reviewed shared store with native expiry, encryption, and deletion; update the public policy accordingly. Cookies require HTTPS even for preview deployments. No user data is sent to optional vendors in the current implementation.
+
+Astro's native hash-based response CSP protects document scripts/styles; middleware preserves that header and appends embedding/resource restrictions. Regular page navigation avoids CSP hash mismatches from client-side route swapping. Do not add unsafe-inline/unsafe-eval script exemptions. Treat content HTML and script edits as trusted code requiring review.
+
+## Technical review outcome (2026-10-14)
+
+The source review removed passive analytics/ad integrations, remote resources, credentialed image requests, article background requests and oversized demo cookies. `pnpm audit --prod` reported no known production dependency vulnerabilities. The integration inventory identifies outstanding hosting/vendor assurance evidence; no independent provider assessment is claimed. Automated HTTP checks cover all article pages as well as public, legal and account pages.
